::STAT_U03_L05_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
Which pattern is exponential?
{
=A. 3, 6, 12, 24#Each value is multiplied by 2.
~B. 3, 6, 9, 12#This adds 3 each time, so it is linear.
~C. 3, 4, 7, 12#This does not use a constant multiplier.
~D. 24, 21, 18, 15#This subtracts 3 each time, so it is linear.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
In y = 40(1.5)ˣ, what is the starting value?
{
=A. 40#The starting value is the coefficient before the growth factor.
~B. 1.5#This is the growth factor, not the starting value.
~C. x#x is the input variable.
~D. 60#This is 40 times 1.5, not the starting value.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
In y = 40(1.5)ˣ, what does 1.5 mean?
{
~A. Add 1.5 each step#Exponential models multiply by the factor each step.
~B. Start at 1.5#The starting value is 40.
~C. Decrease by 50 percent each step#A factor greater than 1 shows growth, not decay.
=D. Multiply by 1.5 each step#The growth factor tells the repeated multiplier.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
In y = 100(0.75)ˣ, what does 0.75 mean?
{
~A. The value increases by 75 percent#A factor less than 1 shows decay.
=B. The value keeps 75 percent each step#Keeping 75 percent means a 25 percent decrease each step.
~C. The starting value is 0.75#The starting value is 100.
~D. Add 0.75 each step#Exponential models multiply, not add, each step.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
Use y = 20(2)ˣ to predict y when x = 3.
{
~A. 60#This multiplies 20 by 3 and ignores the exponent.
~B. 80#This uses 2² instead of 2³.
=C. 160#20(2)³ = 20(8) = 160.
~D. 23#This adds 3 to 20, which is not exponential modeling.
}
